<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pawon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Pawon temple, Candi Pawon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pawon is a small 9th-century Buddhist temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, standing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendut">Mendut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> on a straight line with both. Built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendra dynasty</a>, it is decorated with reliefs of wish-granting trees and celestial beings. Together with Mendut and Borobudur it was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pawon.jpg" alt="Pawon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chainwit. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Candi_Pawon_-_facade_northwest_(2025)_-_img_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Pawon stands about 1.75 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> and 1.15 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendut">Mendut</a>, and with them forms the Borobudur Temple Compounds. The detail and style of its carving suggest that it is slightly older than Borobudur. That the three temples stand on one straight line suggests a symbolic meaning linking them. Its original name is uncertain. Pawon means kitchen in Javanese, a word derived from awu, dust, and this has suggested that it was built as a mortuary temple, a per-awu-an or place of ashes, for a cremated king, though who was laid there is unknown. Local people call it Bajranalan, after the village, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> vajra, thunder or a Buddhist ritual tool, and anala, fire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple stands on a square base and faces slightly north-west. Carved Kala-Makara figures, common in classical Javanese temples, adorn the sides of the stairs and the tops of the gates. The outer walls bear reliefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">bodhisattvas</a> and taras, and of the kalpataru, the tree of life, flanked by kinnara and kinnari. The square chamber inside is empty, with a square basin at its centre, and small rectangular windows probably served for ventilation. Five small stupas and four small ratnas crown the roof.</p><p>For its simplicity, symmetry and harmony historians have called it the jewel of Javanese temple architecture, in contrast with the tall, slender East Javanese temples of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> periods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Yazir Marzuki and Toeti Heraty hold that the temple most probably served to purify the mind before the ascent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a>. Today, at the full moon of May or June, the Buddhist community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> keeps Waisak with a procession on foot that starts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendut">Mendut</a>, passes through Pawon and ends at Borobudur.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pawon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and Kewu plains filled with temples: Kalasan and Sari (778), the 249-structure Manjusrigrha or Sewu complex (792), Mendut, <b>Pawon</b> and Borobudur (completed about 825), Plaosan, and the Shivagrha of Prambanan, the largest Hindu temple ever built in Indonesia, raised by Pikatan in 856 and expanded through Lokapala and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The builders measured in tala, the length of a face from hairline to chin, and a survey of 1977 found the ratio 4:6:9 throughout the monument, as at the nearby temples of <b>Pawon</b> and Mendut.</blockquote><a href="/work/borobudur">Borobudur · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Samaratungga the great stupa-mountain of Borobudur was completed around 825, alongside Mendut and <b>Pawon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sailendra-kingdom">Sailendra Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
